<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["StudioHost.styles.css"]" />
    <!--
      Plain relative paths (not @Assets[...]) for the Studio bundle: it's
      built by `npm run build:blazor-assets` (vite.blazor-assets.config.ts)
      straight into wwwroot/studio, OUTSIDE this project's own asset
      pipeline / fingerprint manifest, and with fixed filenames on purpose
      (see harness/blazor-host/README.md) so this reference never depends on
      manifest regeneration timing relative to the JS build step.
    -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="studio/studio.css" />
    <!--
      honua-studio#5 finding (harness/blazor-host/README.md § Findings,
      hazard 1/3): a plain blocking (NOT type="module") inline script,
      placed here so it runs during head parsing — BEFORE
      _framework/blazor.web.js, which is what actually calls
      history.pushState/replaceState for interactive-circuit navigation.
      studio/mount.js (a deferred module script, necessarily late — it
      needs the built element registry) tried wrapping history.pushState
      itself and the wrap was silently never invoked: blazor.web.js had
      already captured its own reference to the ORIGINAL history.pushState
      by the time the deferred module ran, so Blazor's internal calls never
      passed through the late wrapper. Patching here instead, before
      blazor.web.js exists at all, is what actually observes every
      navigation; it only dispatches a plain DOM event, so studio/mount.js
      still does 100% of the actual work.
    -->
    <script>
      (function () {
        function announce() {
          document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("honua-studio-host-navigation"));
        }
        var originalPushState = history.pushState.bind(history);
        history.pushState = function () {
          var result = originalPushState.apply(history, arguments);
          announce();
          return result;
        };
        var originalReplaceState = history.replaceState.bind(history);
        history.replaceState = function () {
          var result = originalReplaceState.apply(history, arguments);
          announce();
          return result;
        };
        window.addEventListener("popstate", announce);
      })();
    </script>
    <ImportMap />
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body>
    <!--
      honua-studio#5 finding (harness/blazor-host/README.md § Findings,
      hazard 1): deliberately NO <div id="studio-host"> here in the Razor
      markup — see harness/blazor-host-src/mount.ts's mount() for why. Any
      element Blazor's server-rendered output declares (empty or not, inside
      <Routes/> or not) is something its client-side patcher reconciles
      children against on every navigation, and reconciling against an
      "empty" declared target means removing whatever's actually there.
      mount.ts creates its own container node purely client-side instead —
      a node with no corresponding server-rendered counterpart anywhere in
      Blazor's output, which its patcher never touches because it has
      nothing to reconcile it against.
    -->
    <Routes />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <!--
      type="module" is implicitly deferred — runs once, after the initial
      HTML parses, and (the whole point) never re-runs on Blazor's enhanced
      navigation, which patches the DOM client-side rather than reloading
      the page. See harness/blazor-host-src/mount.ts.
    -->
    <script type="module" src="studio/mount.js"></script>
</body>

</html>
